{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-231",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Prism spectrometer: collimation, dispersion and focusing onto one screen. Red and violet slit images remain distinct; drawing coordinates are schematic, not quantitative optics.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-231.png",
      "sha256": "76fd6169249e13561efe6d1018df61a458823a5b818f407767bed6d0ffb16ba8"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:zh:p0277#10+p0278#1",
        "text": "精细研究光谱时，只用一条窄缝和一块棱镜往往不够。\n牛顿使用棱镜和透镜的改进装置（图197），也说明了需要把不同波长的光清楚地分开，尽量减少相邻部分的重叠。\n完成这项工作的仪器叫**光谱仪**，其中常用棱镜或衍射光栅来分光。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:zh:p0278#2+p0278#5",
        "text": "图231是棱镜光谱仪的结构简图。\n光先进入**准直管**：一端是带窄缝的屏，另一端是会聚透镜 $L_1$。\n窄缝放在透镜的前焦平面上，从窄缝发出的发散光通过透镜后，近似成为平行光，再射向棱镜 $P$。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:zh:p0278#4",
        "text": "图 231"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:zh:p0278#6",
        "text": "在棱镜材料中，不同频率的光通常有不同的折射率，因此离开棱镜时沿不同方向传播。\n这些光射到透镜 $L_2$，在它后面的焦平面放置毛玻璃屏或感光板。\n透镜 $L_2$ 把沿各个方向传播的平行光聚焦到屏上的不同位置，形成窄缝的一系列像。\n每一个像对应一个很窄的频率范围，这些像合起来就是光谱。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:zh:p0278#7",
        "text": "用于记录光谱的装置叫**摄谱仪**；用目视望远镜观察分开的光时，叫**分光镜**。\n透镜和棱镜不一定只能用普通玻璃，还可以按所测波段选择石英、岩盐等材料。\n一种材料对可见光透明，不代表它对其他波段也透明；选择光学材料时要考虑其透射和吸收。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:en:p0277#10+p0278#1",
        "text": "A narrow slit and a prism alone are often not enough for a detailed study of spectra.\nNewton's improved prism-and-lens arrangement (Figure 197) also illustrates the need to separate wavelengths clearly and reduce overlap between neighbouring parts.\nAn instrument that does this is a **spectrometer**, often using a prism or diffraction grating to separate the light."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:en:p0278#2+p0278#5",
        "text": "Figure 231 is a schematic of a prism spectrometer.\nLight first enters a **collimator**: a screen with a narrow slit at one end and a converging lens $L_1$ at the other.\nThe slit lies in the lens's front focal plane, so diverging light from the slit becomes approximately parallel after the lens and reaches prism $P$."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:en:p0278#4",
        "text": "Figure 231"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:en:p0278#6",
        "text": "Different frequencies usually have different refractive indices in the prism material, so they leave the prism in different directions.\nThey reach lens $L_2$, with a ground-glass screen or photographic plate in its rear focal plane.\nLens $L_2$ focuses parallel light travelling in different directions at different screen positions, forming a series of slit images.\nEach image corresponds to a narrow frequency range; together, the images form the spectrum."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n104:en:p0278#7",
        "text": "An instrument that records the spectrum is called a **spectrograph**; one that lets us view the separated light through a telescope is a **spectroscope**.\nLenses and prisms need not be made from ordinary glass: quartz, rock salt, and other materials can be selected for the band being measured.\nBeing transparent to visible light does not make a material transparent to every other band, so transmission and absorption matter when choosing optical materials."
      }
    ],
